      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Christopher Jarzynski",
          "Denis J. Evans",
          "Gavin E. Crooks"
        ],
        "citation": "for exact fluctuation theorems and nonequilibrium work relations"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B035"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B004 identifies an unusually clean and universal advance: exact relations connect irreversible trajectories to equilibrium free-energy differences without reliance on a near-equilibrium approximation. Evans supplied essential fluctuation-theorem foundations, Jarzynski the decisive work equality, and Crooks its general fluctuation formulation. The result has been rigorously understood, repeatedly tested in small systems, and has reshaped how statistical physics treats nonequilibrium fluctuations.",
    "case_against": "Its strongest reservation is that the achievement is principally conceptual, with practical and experimental consequences less concentrated than those of major measurement instruments or technologies. The proper historical boundary among fluctuation-theorem precursors and the later experimental literature also warrants careful scrutiny.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that B024 transformed quantitative work on correlated electrons, is broadly benchmarked, and connects microscopic models to real materials, while acknowledging distributed credit.",
        "response": "I agree that dynamical mean-field theory is an exceptionally mature and powerful framework, and it remains my leading alternative. But its intellectual development and later extensions make three-person attribution intrinsically diffuse. B004 has a sharper core mechanism and a more compact chain from foundational theorem to general formulation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that B035 joins Katori's magic-wavelength concept to Ye's pioneering realizations, with independent clock agreement and broad metrological use.",
        "response": "This is a compelling, clearly attributed experimental achievement and has strengthened my view of B035 as an alternative. I nevertheless give B004 priority because its exact relations establish a general physical principle applicable across nonequilibrium systems, rather than a particular high-performance measurement platform whose most consequential future applications remain partly prospective."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson notes that B012 has repeatedly realized coherent operations, entanglement, simulation, and computation, but that its many pioneers complicate three-person credit.",
        "response": "That attribution difficulty is material. Trapped ions are a major platform, but the proposed trio cannot fully represent its experimental and theoretical lineage. The three names in B004 instead correspond directly to closely specified, complementary exact relations."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the conceptual reach of the fluctuation relations should outweigh the more visible technological and metrological impact of leading alternatives.",
      "The precise historical priority and appropriate citation boundary among fluctuation-theorem formulations require further review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No specific conflict is apparent. My profile notes a shared Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but identifies no collaboration with either."
  }
}
